Subject: [PATCH 28/51] tei-v7 prompt-02: Repository-wide contract and caller
 audit

---
 codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py b/codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py
index cba7db1..881f46c 100644
--- a/codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py
+++ b/codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py
@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@ from codemonkeys.prompts.shared_state_machine_prompts import format_files
 FULL_FILE_EXAMPLE = """
 Before final approval, build an executable requirement-to-test matrix from the issue. For every independently stated behavior, identify the implementation location and run an ordinary-case test, a boundary or malformed-input test, and a regression test for the reported failure. Each test must assert the expected behavior—not merely that execution completes—and must exercise the public entry point used by the issue. Mark each requirement as PASS, FAIL, or UNTESTED; never treat UNTESTED or an ambiguous result as approval. Do not approve a patch while any requirement lacks a directly relevant test or while a test result is ambiguous. After each test run, map every failure back to the matrix and either revise the patch or explain why the failure is unrelated.
 
-Before final approval, enumerate every caller, subclass, override, serializer, parser, and public consumer of each modified symbol using repository search. For each consumer, record its expected inputs, outputs, exceptions, ordering, mutation, and compatibility invariant, then verify the patch against that record. If any consumer cannot be located or its behavior is unknown, do not approve the patch: search broader call paths, add a focused regression test, or revise the implementation. Treat changed error types, defaults, ordering, mutation, and serialization as behavior changes requiring explicit justification.
+Before final approval, enumerate every caller, subclass, override, serializer, parser, and public consumer of each modified symbol using repository search, including indirect callers discovered through imports and dispatch tables. For each consumer, trace one representative input through the modified behavior and verify that the patch preserves its expected return type, exceptions, ordering, mutation, defaults, and backward compatibility. If any consumer has not been checked, do not approve the patch. For each consumer, record its expected inputs, outputs, exceptions, ordering, mutation, and compatibility invariant, then verify the patch against that record. If any consumer cannot be located or its behavior is unknown, do not approve the patch: search broader call paths, add a focused regression test, or revise the implementation. Treat changed error types, defaults, ordering, mutation, and serialization as behavior changes requiring explicit justification.
 
 When comparing alternative patches, first create a table with one row per issue requirement and one column per candidate. For each cell, cite the specific changed code and test result that supports or contradicts the requirement; mark missing evidence as unsupported rather than inferring support from unrelated passing tests. Reject any candidate with an unsupported requirement, a newly failing existing test, unexplained test failures, or unrelated edits. Only after all requirements are supported should test counts, patch size, and model preference break ties; prefer the smallest behaviorally complete candidate.
 
-- 
2.39.5 (Apple Git-154)

